## Culprit: `crime_00022` — Sync I/O in Hot Path (`src/api/files.ts`)

Quoted evidence:

> - `` `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - lines: 12, 13, 15
> - swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it

**Sync methods involved:** `fs.existsSync`, `fs.statSync`, `fs.readFileSync` — all three run on every request inside `loadPlanSchema`, blocking the event loop for the full disk-read duration. Under concurrent load that's exactly the shape of the symptom (event-loop lag, p99 blowout).

## Smallest async migration (preserves public signature shape)

Mark `loadPlanSchema` `async` and switch to `fs/promises`. The signature stays the same except for the `Promise<…>` wrapper, which is how async migrations are required to surface in TS:

```ts
// before
import { existsSync, statSync, readFileSync } from "node:fs";

export function loadPlanSchema(path: string): PlanSchema {
  if (!existsSync(path)) throw new Error(`missing: ${path}`);
  const stat = statSync(path);
  if (!stat.isFile()) throw new Error(`not a file: ${path}`);
  const raw = readFileSync(path, "utf8");
  return JSON.parse(raw) as PlanSchema;
}

// after
import { readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(path: string): Promise<PlanSchema> {
  let s;
  try {
    s = await stat(path);
  } catch (err: any) {
    if (err.code === "ENOENT") throw new Error(`missing: ${path}`);
    throw err;
  }
  if (!s.isFile()) throw new Error(`not a file: ${path}`);
  const raw = await readFile(path, "utf8");
  return JSON.parse(raw) as PlanSchema;
}
```

Notes on the minimal change:
- `existsSync` + `statSync` collapse into a single `await stat()` with an `ENOENT` catch — one syscall instead of two, and no extra blocking.
- `readFileSync` → `await readFile(path, "utf8")`.
- Callers must now `await loadPlanSchema(...)`; the parameter list and return shape are unchanged.

Per the scenario constraint: this fixes the blocking call itself rather than caching the result to hide it. (If you later want to cache, do it on top of the async version — but the latency bug is the sync syscalls, not repeated reads.)
